diff --git a/patches/openwrt/0040-kernel-6.6-report-dying-CPU-to-RCU-from-ipi_cpu_stop.patch b/patches/openwrt/0040-kernel-6.6-report-dying-CPU-to-RCU-from-ipi_cpu_stop.patch
new file mode 100644
index 00000000..f094b050
--- /dev/null
+++ b/patches/openwrt/0040-kernel-6.6-report-dying-CPU-to-RCU-from-ipi_cpu_stop.patch
@@ -0,0 +1,72 @@
+From 4a7c38bdbfa8f133ce6924e0990b6950f4990a31 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
+From: Florian Maurer <fmaurer@disroot.org>
+Date: Tue, 19 May 2026 23:35:09 +0000
+Subject: [PATCH] kernel: 6.6: report dying CPU to RCU from ipi_cpu_stop()
+
+ARM smp_send_stop() bypasses normal CPU-hotplug teardown: ipi_cpu_stop()
+clears cpu_online_mask but never calls rcu_report_dead(), leaving RCU's
+per-CPU bitmasks stale. This causes a ~21s RCU stall and a WARN in
+irq_work_queue_on() on poweroff/halt on dual-core IPQ806x (e.g. AP-325).
+
+Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.7 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
+---
+ ...call-rcu_report_dead-in-ipi_cpu_stop.patch | 47 +++++++++++++++++++
+ 1 file changed, 47 insertions(+)
+ create mode 100644 target/linux/generic/pending-6.6/311-ARM-smp-call-rcu_report_dead-in-ipi_cpu_stop.patch
+
+diff --git a/target/linux/generic/pending-6.6/311-ARM-smp-call-rcu_report_dead-in-ipi_cpu_stop.patch b/target/linux/generic/pending-6.6/311-ARM-smp-call-rcu_report_dead-in-ipi_cpu_stop.patch
+new file mode 100644
+index 0000000000..560bd231d9
+--- /dev/null
++++ b/target/linux/generic/pending-6.6/311-ARM-smp-call-rcu_report_dead-in-ipi_cpu_stop.patch
+@@ -0,0 +1,47 @@
++From: Florian Maurer <fmaurer@disroot.org>
++Subject: ARM: smp: call rcu_report_dead() in ipi_cpu_stop()
++
++When the system is halted or powered off, machine_halt() and
++machine_power_off() use smp_send_stop() to take secondary CPUs offline.
++The IPI handler ipi_cpu_stop() clears the dying CPU from cpu_online_mask
++via set_cpu_online(cpu, false) and then spins forever in
++"cpu_relax(); wfe();" from interrupt context.
++
++This bypasses the normal CPU-hotplug teardown path: rcu_report_dead()
++and rcutree_offline_cpu() are never called, so RCU's qsmaskinitnext and
++ffmask bits for the stopped CPU remain set. Meanwhile cpu_is_offline()
++returns true. The two views disagree.
++
++If the RCU grace-period kthread had armed a schedule_timeout() timer on
++the stopped CPU just before the IPI arrived, the timer can never be
++serviced (the CPU is wedged in IRQ context with IRQs masked), so the
++kthread starves until CONFIG_RCU_CPU_STALL_TIMEOUT fires. The stall
++detector then kicks the kthread alive on another CPU, where
++force_qs_rnp() -> rcu_implicit_dynticks_qs() reaches an
++irq_work_queue_on() targeting the offline CPU and trips
++WARN_ON_ONCE(cpu_is_offline(cpu)) in kernel/irq_work.c.
++
++Reproduced on Aruba AP-325 (Qualcomm IPQ8068, dual-core Krait, 6.6.x)
++by issuing "poweroff": 21-second stall followed by the WARN.
++
++Call rcu_report_dead(cpu) before the wait loop so that RCU's per-CPU
++bitmasks match the now-offline state. This reports a quiescent state on
++behalf of the stopping CPU, releases the current grace period, and
++prevents the subsequent irq_work_queue_on() from being attempted.
++
++Signed-off-by: Florian Maurer <fmaurer@disroot.org>
++---
++ arch/arm/kernel/smp.c | 2 ++
++ 1 file changed, 2 insertions(+)
++
++--- a/arch/arm/kernel/smp.c
+++++ b/arch/arm/kernel/smp.c
++@@ -608,6 +608,8 @@ static void ipi_cpu_stop(unsigned int cp
++
++ 	set_cpu_online(cpu, false);
++
+++	rcu_report_dead(cpu);
+++
++ 	while (1) {
++ 		cpu_relax();
++ 		wfe();
+-- 
+2.51.2
+
